#>swap_grace
// Swap Grace
#
// Operator and boundary swapping is an invited mechanic, not an error mode.
// This surface names the law that makes swapping safe: alternate forms,
// cursor-preserving substitution, and disclosed (never silent) deltas.
#
// @see docs/theory/spw/operational-topography.spw
// @see docs/theory/spw/coupling-constructors.spw
// @see docs/theory/spw/form-geometry.spw
// @see docs/theory/spw/form-contours.spw
// @see packages/spw-seed/src/types/coupling.ts
// @see packages/spw-seed/src/canonical/form-geometry.ts
// @see packages/spw-runtime/src/state/liminality-bridge.ts

#:layer #!semantics

^seed[Spw.Swap.Grace v:0.1 @profile:proposed @intent:safe_exploratory_substitution]
{_swap_grace

 ## Status Vocabulary

 status: .{
  implemented = `directly observable in parser, runtime, CLI, or LSP code`
  measured = `recomputable from named inputs with a published method`
  proposed = `a typed contract or experiment that is not yet runtime law`
  interpretive = `a local rendering, metaphor, or human report`
 }[reg=facet]

 rule: `This surface reuses operational-topography's four-value status vocabulary rather than restating a local variant.`

 ## Motivation

 premise: `Swapping operators, sigils, and boundary spellings is an intended creative mechanic — it produces diff wonder. Mistakes made this way should carry the same grace a kitchen gives an ingredient substitution: bounded, disclosed, and recoverable, not a cliff.`
 failure_mode: `A swap is graceless when its semantic delta is undeclared, so neither a human nor an agent can tell in advance whether the swap is cosmetic or meaning-changing.`

 ## Cursor

 status: proposed

 definition: `A cursor is the addressable position of one named value in the language's structural lattice: where it sits, what shape holds it, and what it carries.`

 shape: .{
  label = `the named value this cursor tracks`
  coupling = `either a paired-boundary kind (frame, body, scope, capsule, stream, nrange) or the couple operator — never conflate operator form with boundary form`
  boundary_kind = `when coupling is a boundary: one of frame|body|scope|capsule|stream|nrange (seed COUPLING_DESCRIPTORS)`
  operator_kind = `when coupling is an operator: couple (surface <>) — not a boundary_kind`
  liminal_stage = `optional runtime LIMINALITY_ORDER stage (local, liminal, visible, global) from packages/spw-runtime — only when the cursor projects into a RegisterBank`
  surface_form = `the literal spelling used at this position (a sigil, brace pair, or keyword)`
  payload = `optional cargo satisfying operational-topography's payload contract (schema, units, serialization, provenance)`
 }[reg=facet]

 axes_separated: .{
  source_position = `where the glyph sits in the source lattice (label + coupling + surface_form) — parser/seed concern`
  runtime_liminality = `RegisterBank visibility stage after an explicit effect.l1.memory bridge call — runtime concern`
  law = `Parsing or rewriting surface text never itself advances liminal_stage; only bridgeRegisterLiminality / applyRegisterBridgeEffect may.`
 }[reg=facet]

 rule_single_axis: `Surface-syntax waypoint vocabularies (e.g. form-geometry's exterior, void, chamber, hole, aperture, membrane, published) name source_position roles, not runtime liminality. Any rule that terminates at a named surface waypoint must declare which liminal_stage (if any) that waypoint projects onto after an explicit bridge; silence means "not yet mapped," not "not applicable."`

 declared_projection: .{
  surface_published = `runtime global`
  source = `packages/spw-runtime/src/state/liminality-bridge.ts`
  status = implemented
  counterexample = `Any other surface waypoint claimed to equal a runtime liminality stage without a declared projection like this one.`
 }[reg=facet]

 ## Recursive Presence

 status: proposed

 rule: `A cursor's payload may itself be a cursor. Nesting must never reduce a nested cursor's own position, payload, or rule history to opacity — every cursor remains independently inspectable at any depth.`

 distinction: `"Recursive" describes the nesting structure; "presence" is the added guarantee that nesting does not hide. A reduced view (see form-contours.spw) may summarize a deep cursor tree, but the underlying tree itself never loses a cursor's presence — only a view does, and only with a receipt.`

 composition_note: `Whether $(label) may appear as a nested payload rather than only a whole-source form is gated by whether its owning boundary's payload schema admits a cursor-shaped payload. This is not yet declared for register_meta sites; treat nested $(label) as unsupported until a boundary kind explicitly admits it.`

 ## Alternate Forms

 status: proposed

 definition: `Two surface strings are alternate forms of one another iff they project to the same (label, coupling, surface role) source_position and neither triggers a distinct runtime effect on parse alone.`

 law_grace_swap: `Substituting one alternate form for another is always grace-safe: the result is syntactically valid, the source_position is unchanged, and no runtime effect fires from the substitution alone.`

 law_not_alternate: `A rewrite that changes boundary_kind, operator_kind, or (after bridge) liminal_stage is a real move (re-typing or promotion), not an alternate form, even when the surface strings look similar. It must carry its own effect grade and inverse, the way form-geometry's mobility rules already do.`

 worked_example_couple_capsule: .{
  status = implemented
  observation = `packages/spw-seed/src/types/coupling.ts declares <> as the couple operator (kind=couple, form=operator) and < > (spaced) as the empty Capsule boundary (kind=capsule, form=boundary) — same visual family, deliberately different coupling identities, distinguished by spacing rather than by a different glyph.`
  reading = `This is not an alternate-forms pair — it is the disclosed opposite case: two near-identical spellings that must NOT be swapped without changing meaning. Swap grace requires this distinction to be look-up-able, not memorized.`
 }[reg=facet]

 worked_example_register_meta: .{
  status = implemented
  observation = `form-geometry.ts's promote.register_bridge accepts only ref_handle (@(L)) as input, not header (^["L"]{}) forms, specifically because a header may carry a payload that $(L) has no slot for.`
  reading = `@(L) and $(L) are different surface roles (ref_handle vs register_meta). Writing $(L) never itself promotes a RegisterBank cell — only an explicit effect.l1.memory bridge call (bridgeRegisterLiminality) may. Grace comes from that split, not from treating @ and $ as cosmetic alternate forms.`
 }[reg=facet]

 ## Boundary-Form Consistency

 status: proposed

 rule: `Each paired-boundary kind declares its own admissible set of surface_forms. seed COUPLING_DESCRIPTORS and coupling-constructors includedKinds / local-brace-vocabulary disclosure are this declaration already in practice; this surface names it as a general law rather than a per-profile note.`

 default_forms: .{
  frame = `[] / […]  (seed emptySurface [] — not {})`
  body = `{} / {…}`
  scope = `() / (…)`
  capsule = `<…> / empty < >`
  stream = `<<…>>`
  nrange = `((…))`
  couple_operator = `<>  (operator form — not a boundary)`
 }[reg=facet]

 default_form: `Where a boundary_kind has a historical brace-family spelling, that spelling is the declared default alternate form (Frame defaults to []; Body defaults to {}). Generalizing to additional coverage must not strand the default form's legibility — new kinds add options, they do not demote the incumbent.`

 counterexample: `A boundary_kind whose admissible surface_forms are not declared anywhere a reader or analyzer can query them.`
 counterexample_frame_body: `Calling {} Frame's historical spelling — Body owns {}; Frame owns [].`

 ## Inspectability

 rule_receipt_parity: `A cursor's payload provenance is a receipt in the same sense form-contours.ts uses for reduction: it names what produced the value and can be traced back, not just displayed forward.`

 rule_swap_visibility: `A diff that swaps between two surface_forms of the same source_position should be legible as one named event ("alternate form, no semantic delta") rather than an anonymous character-level change. A diff that changes coupling or liminal_stage should be equally legible as a real move, distinguishing which coordinate changed.`

 not_yet_built: `No analyzer currently classifies a diff hunk this way. spw-topography-scan.ts measures container-kind counts and parse health across real files but has no cursor-diff or swap-group notion yet.`

 ## Falsification

 falsify: #[
  "A surface waypoint is treated as equal to a runtime liminality stage with no declared_projection entry naming the correspondence.",
  "Two surface_forms are called alternate forms but produce different runtime effects on parse alone.",
  "A nested cursor's position or payload becomes unrecoverable through a reduction that carries no receipt.",
  "A boundary_kind's admissible surface_forms are asserted but not queryable from any doc, type, or profile.",
  "An alternate-form substitution triggers effect.l1.memory or a workspace write without an explicit separate call.",
  "Couple operator <> is classified as a boundary_kind or empty capsule without spacing distinction.",
  "Frame is documented with {} as its default empty surface (that empty surface is []).",
 ][reg=set]

}_swap_grace
